{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "lem:independent-calendar-selector-control",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "Let \\(h_s^{\\mathrm{iid}}\\) be selected from the dyadic grid starting at \\(h_{s,\\mathrm{iid}}\\) in \\texttt{def:feasible-estimator}.  For a sufficiently large fixed \\(C_h\\), uniformly over \\(P\\in\\mathcal M_{N,T,\\mathbf n}^{\\dagger,\\mathrm{iid}}(\\lambda)\\), every cohort and every integer \\(v\\ge0\\) with \\(2^vh_{s,\\mathrm{iid}}\\le1\\) satisfy\n\\[\nP\\{h_s^{\\mathrm{iid}}>2^vh_{s,\\mathrm{iid}}\\mid E_0,\\widetilde z_s\\}\n\\le C\\exp(-c2^{vd}).\n\\]\nFor every fixed \\(a>0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_P[(h_s^{\\mathrm{iid}})^a\\mathbf1_{\\mathrm{good,iid}}\\mid E_0,\\widetilde z_s]\n\\le C\\{(Tn_s)^{-a/(2\\lambda+d)}+T^{-a/d}+N^{-a/2}\\}.\n\\]\nThe terminal-grid failure probability is at most \\(C\\exp(-cT)\\), and the iid bad-sample probability is at most \\(CR_\\star\\) along arrays with \\(N\\wedge T\\wedge\\min_s n_s\\to\\infty\\).  No logarithmic block length, dense-local-site condition, or upper local-mass condition is used.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "hsel_s_iid",
      "h_s_iid",
      "s",
      "C_h",
      "P",
      "M_dagger_iid",
      "N",
      "T",
      "n_s",
      "lambda",
      "d",
      "E_0",
      "tilde_z_s",
      "R_star"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:panel-law-class",
      "def:feasible-estimator",
      "def:honest-interval",
      "def:rate-frontier",
      "ass:design-density",
      "ass:donor-independence",
      "ass:donor-fourth-moment"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Condition on \\((E_0,\\widetilde z_s=z)\\).  Donor independence preserves the historical-design law, and the subclass definition makes the calendar vectors independent.  For \\(\\ell_{z,h}\\) and \\(\\Phi_{z,h}\\) defined in the beta-selector proof, the same density, kernel, cube-geometry, and polynomial arguments give\n\\[\nc_0h^d\\le\\mathbb E\\ell_{z,h}(X_{st})\\le C_0h^d,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E[v^\\top\\Phi_{z,h}(X_{st})v]\\ge c_0h^d\n\\]\nuniformly over unit vectors \\(v\\).  Each summand is bounded and its variance is at most \\(Ch^d\\).  For a centered bounded variable \\(V\\), expansion of the exponential series gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb Ee^{\\theta V}\n\\le\\exp\\left\\{\\frac{\\theta^2\\operatorname{Var}(V)}{2(1-C|\\theta|)}\\right\\}\n\\]\nfor sufficiently small \\(|\\theta|\\).  Multiplication over the independent calendar indices and exponential Markov optimization yield\n\\[\nP\\left(\\left|\\sum_{t=1}^T\\{F(X_{st})-\\mathbb EF(X_{st})\\}\\right|>cTh^d\n\\,\\middle|\\,E_0,z\\right)\\le C\\exp(-cTh^d)\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\nfor \\(F=\\ell_{z,h}\\) and for every fixed quadratic form of \\(\\Phi_{z,h}\\).  A fixed sphere net turns (19) into simultaneous mass and Gram bounds.  Replication cancels from \\(G_s/L_s\\), so\n\\[\nP\\{\\text{candidate }h\\text{ fails}\\mid E_0,z\\}\n\\le C\\exp(-cTh^d).\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nPut \\(h_0=h_{s,\\mathrm{iid}}\\).  The term \\(T^{-1/d}\\) ensures\n\\[\nT(2^vh_0)^d\\ge c2^{vd}.\n\\]\nIf the selector has not stopped by \\(2^vh_0\\), that candidate failed, so (20) gives the stated tail; setting \\(h=1\\) gives \\(Ce^{-cT}\\).  Tail summation and \\(\\sum_v2^{av}e^{-c2^{vd}}<\\infty\\) give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[(h_s^{\\mathrm{iid}})^a\\mathbf1_{\\mathrm{good,iid}}\\mid E_0,z]\n\\le Ch_0^a\n\\le C\\{(Tn_s)^{-a/(2\\lambda+d)}+T^{-a/d}+N^{-a/2}\\}.\n\\]\nA union bound over fixed \\(S\\), \\(e^{-cT}\\le CT^{-2\\lambda/d}\\), and the deterministic eventual inequality \\(\\delta_N\\le\\delta_0\\) prove the bad-event bound by \\(CR_\\star\\).  This proves every claim directly from independent bounded concentration.",
    "justification": "Direct bounded concentration over independent calendar vectors gives the no-log dyadic tail and bandwidth moments.",
    "gap": "It applies only to the named conditional independent-calendar subclass.",
    "consumer": "Both iid upper-rate corollaries use it.",
